"Algebraic tangent cones of reflexive sheaves (https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.02172)\nciteturn1academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.2 years ago; Authors: Xuemiao Chen, Song Sun ... We study the notion of algebraic tangent cones at singularities of reflexive sheaves.\nTitle: Algebraic tangent cones of reflexive sheaves\nAuthors: Xuemiao Chen, Song Sun\nDate: Tue Aug  7 01:05:04 2018\n\nWe study the notion of algebraic tangent cones at singularities of reflexive sheaves. These correspond to extensions of reflexive sheaves across a negative divisor. We show the existence of optimal extensions in a constructive manner, and we prove the uniqueness in a suitable sense. The results here are an algebro-geometric counterpart of our previous study on singularities of Hermitian-Yang-Mills connections.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAlgebraic tangent cones of reflexive sheaves | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/1808.02172)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; @misc{chen2018algebraictangentconesreflexive, title={Algebraic tangent cones of reflexive sheaves}, author={Xuemiao Chen and Song Sun}, year={2018}, eprint={1808.02172}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.DG}, doi={https://doi.org/10.1093/imrn/rny276}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.02172}, }\nSubmitted 07 Aug 2018\n\n# Algebraic tangent cones of reflexive sheaves\n\nXC\n\nXuemiao Chen\n\nSS\n\nSong Sun\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study the notion of algebraic tangent cones at singularities of reflexive sheaves. These correspond to extensions of reflexive sheaves across a negative divisor. We show the existence of optimal extensions in a constructive manner, and we prove the uniqueness in a suitable sense. The results here are an algebro-geometric counterpart of our previous study on singularities of Hermitian-Yang-Mills connections.\n\nView Paper\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{chen2018algebraictangentconesreflexive, title={Algebraic tangent cones of reflexive sheaves}, author={Xuemiao Chen and Song Sun}, year={2018}, eprint={1808.02172}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.DG}, doi={https://doi.org/10.1093/imrn/rny276}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.02172}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA simple proof of a theorem of Albert (https://scispace.com/pdf/a-simple-proof-of-a-theorem-of-albert-3llhozzjfh.pdf)\nciteturn1search13 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; ** A simple proof is given of the following theorem of Albert: An associative division algebra of degree 4 over its center is of order 4 in the Brauer group if and only if it cannot be written as a tensor product of quaternion algebras. ... ** *A central division algebra* 𝒟/F *of degree 4 has an involution of the first kind if and only if* 𝒟 = 𝒬₁ ⊗₍F₎ 𝒬₂, 𝒬ᵢ *quaternion algebras over* F.\nPROCEEDINGS OF THE AMERICAN MATHEMATICAL SOCIETY  \nVolume 43, Number 2, April 1974\n\n# A SIMPLE PROOF OF A THEOREM OF ALBERT\n\nM. L. RACINE¹\n\n**Abstract.** A simple proof is given of the following theorem of Albert: An associative division algebra of degree 4 over its center is of order 4 in the Brauer group if and only if it cannot be written as a tensor product of quaternion algebras.\n\nThe following theorem is stated without proof in [3, Theorem 11.2], where the reader is referred to [1] and [2].\n\n**Theorem 1.** *Let* 𝒟/F *be a central division algebra of degree 4.* 𝒟 *has order 4 in the Brauer group if and only if it cannot be written as a tensor product of quaternion algebras.*\n\nIn this note we show that this follows easily from other (by no means trivial) results of Albert to be found in [3]. Albert’s original proof is involved and not readily accessible to the modern reader. First recall that a finite dimensional division algebra has an involution of the first kind if and only if it is of order 1 or 2 in the Brauer group [3, Theorem 10.19]. Since 𝒟 has order 2 or 4 in the Brauer group, Theorem 1 is equivalent to\n\n**Theorem 2.** *A central division algebra* 𝒟/F *of degree 4 has an involution of the first kind if and only if* 𝒟 = 𝒬₁ ⊗₍F₎ 𝒬₂, 𝒬ᵢ *quaternion algebras over* F.\n\n**Proof.** Let 𝒟/F be a central division algebra of degree 4. Then 𝒟 contains a separable quadratic field extension E/F [3, Theorem 11.9]. Therefore E = F(x) for some x ∈ 𝒟 such that x² − x + β = 0, β ∈ F. By the Skolem-Noether theorem the automorphism of E/F mapping x into 1 − x can be extended to an automorphism σ of 𝒟/F and a^σ = c⁻¹ac, ∀a ∈ 𝒟, for some fixed c ∈ 𝒟. Assume that 𝒟 has an involution of the first kind. Then 𝒟 has an involution * of the first kind such that * is the identity on E [3, Theorem 10.15]. We wish to construct an involution of the first kind which maps x into 1 − x. Consider the map φ: 𝒟 → 𝒟 given by a^φ = c⁻¹a* c, a ∈ 𝒟. If c* = ±c this is an involution. Suppose\n\nReceived by the editors July 10, 1973.  \nAMS (MOS) subject classifications (1970). Primary 16A28, 16A40.  \n¹ The author is a Postdoctoral Fellow of the National Research Council of Canada.\n\n© American Mathematical Society 1974\n\n487--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nX.M. Chen and S. Sun (2018) “Algebraic Tangent Cones of Reflexive Sheaves,” (https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10188058)\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Sun (2018) “Algebraic Tangent Cones of Reflexive Sheaves,” ... Xuemiao Chen1,∗and Song Sun1,2 ... We study the notion of algebraic tangent cones at singularities of reflexive sheaves. ... sheaf over B.\nX.M. Chen and S. Sun (2018) “Algebraic Tangent Cones of Reflexive Sheaves,”\nInternational Mathematics Research Notices, Vol. 00, No. 0, pp. 1–22\nAdvance Access publication on Month XX, 2018\ndoi:10.1093/imrn/rny276\nAlgebraic Tangent Cones of Reflexive Sheaves\nXuemiao Chen1,∗and Song Sun1,2\n1Stony Brook University, Mathematics, 11794, USA, and 2University of\nCalifornia, Berkeley, Mathematics, 94720\n∗Correspondence to be sent to: e-mail: xuemiaochen1991@gmail.com\nWe study the notion of algebraic tangent cones at singularities of reflexive sheaves.\nThese correspond to extensions of reflexive sheaves across a negative divisor. We\nshow the existence of optimal extensions in a constructive manner, and we prove the\nuniqueness in a suitable sense. The results here are an algebro-geometric counterpart\nof our previous study on singularities of Hermitian–Yang–Mills connections.\n1\nIntroduction\nThe goal of this paper is to study a complex-algebraic object that comes out of our study\nof singularities of Hermitian–Yang–Mills connections [1, 2]. The discussion here will be\npurely complex-algebraic, and the connection with the previous results will be given by\nConjecture 1.7. Let B ⊂Cn be the unit ball, and let E be a reflexive analytic coherent\nsheaf over B. Let ˆB be the blow-up of B at 0. We will use the following notation:\n•\nπ : B\\{0} →CPn−1 and π′ : Cn \\{0} →CPn−1 are the natural projection maps;\n•\nψ : B \\ {0} →B and ψ′ : Cn \\ {0} →Cn are the natural inclusion maps;\n•\np : ˆB →B and φ : ˆB →CPn−1 are the natural projection maps;\n•\nD := p−1(0) is the exceptional divisor, and ι : D →ˆB is the natural inclusion\nmap.\nDownloaded from https://academic.oup.com/imrn/advance-article/doi/10.1093/imrn/rny276/5230062 by guest on 29 August 2020\nReceived August 13, 2018; Revised August 13, 2018; Accepted November 5, 2018\n© The Author(s) 2018. Published by Oxford University Press. All rights reserved.\nFor permissions, please e-mail: journals.permission@oup.com.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAMS :: Proceedings of the American Mathematical Society (https://www.ams.org/proc/2002-130-12/S0002-9939-02-06746-1/)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 24.3 years ago; Crawled: 1.3 years ago; We provide a short proof of the well-known fact that a division ring of finite degre